# Efficient Quantum Algorithms for Simulating Lindblad Evolution

**Richard Cleve, Chunhao Wang** \\
Institute for Quantum Computing and Cheriton School of Computer Science, \\
University of Waterloo; Canadian Institute for Advanced Research. \\
arXiv:1612.09512v3 [quant-ph] 4 Jan 2019. Preliminary version ICALP 2017.

## 1. Lindblad master equation (Eq. 1)

\[
    \dot\rho \;=\; -i [H,\rho] \;+\; \sum_{j=1}^{m}\!\Bigl(L_j\,\rho\,L_j^{\dagger}
        - \tfrac{1}{2} L_j^{\dagger} L_j\,\rho
        - \tfrac{1}{2}\,\rho\, L_j^{\dagger} L_j \Bigr).
\]

## Theorem 1 (Pauli-LCU Lindbladian)

Let \(\mathcal{L}\) be a Lindbladian presented as a linear combination of q Pauli-tensor terms, with m Lindblad operators. Then for any time \(t\) and precision \(\varepsilon > 0\), the Lindblad evolution \(e^{t\mathcal{L}}\) can be simulated by a quantum circuit of gate complexity

\[
    O\bigl(\tau\;\mathrm{polylog}(mq\tau/\varepsilon)\;\mathrm{poly}(n)\bigr),
\]

where \(\tau = t\,\lVert L\rVert_{\mathrm{pauli}}\).

## Corollary 2 (local Lindbladian)

If \(\mathcal{L}\) is a local Lindbladian, the gate complexity for simulating \(e^{t\mathcal{L}}\) with precision \(\varepsilon\) is
\[
    O\bigl(t\,\mathrm{polylog}(t/\varepsilon)\,\mathrm{poly}(n)\bigr).
\]

## Corollary 3 (d-sparse Lindbladian)

The query complexity and gate complexity for simulating d-sparse Lindbladians \(\mathcal L\) are
\[
    O\bigl(\tau\,\mathrm{polylog}(mq\tau/\varepsilon)\,\mathrm{poly}(d,n)\bigr).
\]

## Impossibility of naive Stinespring-dilation shortcut

Naively realising Lindblad evolution as Hamiltonian evolution on a system+ancilla dilation, resetting the ancilla every \(t/N\), incurs an overhead of

\[
    O(t^2 / \varepsilon)
\]

*before* the Hamiltonian simulation itself. This rules out achieving the theorem's polylog(1/ε) scaling via any straightforward dilation-based reduction.

## Comparison / prior work quantifiers

| Algorithm | Query / gate complexity |
|-----------|--------------------------|
| Kliesch et al. (earlier) | O(t^2 / \varepsilon) |
| Childs–Li 2016 [9] | O(t^{1.5} / \sqrt{\varepsilon}) and O((t^2/\varepsilon)\,\mathrm{polylog}(t/\varepsilon)) |
| Lower bound (H=0, m=1) [9] | \Omega(t) |
| **This paper (Thm 1)** | \(O(t\,\mathrm{polylog}(t/\varepsilon)\,\mathrm{poly}(n))\) |

## Novel LCU-for-channels ingredient (§3)

The construction introduces a purifier register whose subsequent measurement realises the Kraus-branch selection of the channel, avoiding the coherent-post-selection failure mode of the standard LCU applied to a Stinespring dilation. The success probability of the new construction (Eq. 16-derived) exceeds that of standard LCU applied to a dilation, which is the mathematical reason polylog(1/ε) survives.

## Truncated-Taylor / LCU expansion (the object the replication script exercises)

\[
    e^{\delta \mathcal L} \;\approx\; \sum_{k=0}^{K} \frac{(\delta\mathcal L)^k}{k!},
\qquad K = O(\log(1/\varepsilon) / \log\log(1/\varepsilon)),
\]

with \(\delta = t/N\) segments and oblivious amplitude amplification per segment.
